      "model_name": "claude-opus-5-5",
      "message": "I've submitted predictions for all 30 evaluation designs. Nothing comes back to score them against, so I can't tell how well they did.\n\n**How I ran the experiments:** I used all 10 to pin down a hyperbolic discounting model, where a delayed reward is worth `dR / (1 + k·D)` today. The results bracket k between about 0.0125 and 0.0135 per day:\n- The person took the delayed reward for 650 vs 1000 after 40 days, which puts k below 0.0135.\n- They took the immediate reward for 10 vs 20 after 80 days, which puts k above 0.0125.\n- The other eight outcomes all fit inside that range, and none contradicted each other.\n\n**How I made the predictions:** I fitted a Bayesian model over k and a noise level for the choices. It also allows a 2% chance that the person picks at random. The probability of picking the delayed reward was averaged over everything the data allowed.\n\n**What came out:** Most of the evaluation designs have long delays and only modest increases in reward, so the model expects the immediate reward. Those get probabilities of about 6–12%. A few clearly favour waiting and get high probabilities:\n- 28 vs 187 after 107 days: 0.95\n- 56 vs 126 after 28 days: 0.93\n- 37 vs 175 after 264 days: 0.72\n\nThree were borderline and got values in between (0.19–0.28).\n\n**Main limitation:** 10 binary choices tell you little about how noisy the person is. The predictions also depend on the hyperbolic form being right, and none of the experiments tested that.",
